@meta
id: 31-05
title: Lists, and why vim.opt is not vim.o
chapter: 31
type: lesson
skills: lua-config
par: 115
minutes: 6
vscode: no equivalent — JSON settings have no +=
open: init.lua

@brief
Not every vim option is a boolean or a number. Some are comma-separated
lists (`wildmode`, `listchars`, `clipboard`, `completeopt`), and in a
vimrc you grow them with `set option+=value` rather than assigning the
whole thing and clobbering what was there.

`vim.opt` exists to make that work in Lua. It gives you a wrapper object
rather than a raw value, so a list option can be written as a Lua table
and can be grown with a method:

    set wildmode=longest,full   ->  vim.opt.wildmode = { 'longest', 'full' }
    set shortmess+=c            ->  vim.opt.shortmess:append('c')
    set clipboard-=unnamed      ->  vim.opt.clipboard:remove('unnamed')

There is a second table, `vim.o`, which sets the same options as plain
values: `vim.o.wildmode = 'longest,full'` works and is marginally faster.
The difference only bites on lists — `vim.o.wildmode = { ... }` assigns a
table to something that wants a string and errors at startup, and `vim.o`
has no `:append`. The rule that keeps you out of trouble: use `vim.opt`
everywhere, and reach for `vim.o` only when you are reading a value back.

Add three lines to the config, in this order, from these vimrc lines:

    set scrolloff=8
    set wildmode=longest,full
    set shortmess+=c

Graded in vim. You are writing the Lua, not running it.

@keys
| `vim.opt.x = { 'a', 'b' }` | a list option, as a Lua table |
| `vim.opt.x:append('v')` | the Lua spelling of `set x+=v` |
| `G` `o` | open a line at the bottom |

@hints
Two assignments and one method call. Only the `+=` line becomes a method
call.
--
Lua tables use braces and commas: `{ 'longest', 'full' }`. Single quotes
and double quotes are the same thing in Lua.
--
`Go`, then the three lines with Enter between them, then Esc and `:wq`.

@solution
    G o
    vim.opt.scrolloff = 8<Enter>
    vim.opt.wildmode = { 'longest', 'full' }<Enter>
    vim.opt.shortmess:append('c')<Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

The colon in `:append` is Lua's method-call syntax — it passes the object
on the left as the first argument, the same way `obj.method(obj, 'c')`
would. You do not need to care beyond writing it correctly, but it does
explain why it is `:append` and not `.append`.

Three practical notes:

- `vim.opt` also has `:remove()` and `:get()`. `vim.opt.wildmode:get()`
  hands you back a Lua table, which is how you inspect an option from a
  script rather than reading it off `:set wildmode?`.
- Window- and buffer-local options have their own tables: `vim.opt_local`,
  `vim.wo` and `vim.bo`. `setlocal spell` is `vim.opt_local.spell = true`,
  and that is what goes in an `after/ftplugin/markdown.lua`.
- Vim's own `set` is still there. `vim.cmd('set wildmode=longest,full')`
  runs verbatim, and is a perfectly respectable way to postpone porting a
  line you are unsure about.

@start init.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua

-- Display
vim.opt.number = true
vim.opt.shiftwidth = 2

@expected init.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua

-- Display
vim.opt.number = true
vim.opt.shiftwidth = 2
vim.opt.scrolloff = 8
vim.opt.wildmode = { 'longest', 'full' }
vim.opt.shortmess:append('c')
